the 1,300 meter long eastside gallery is  the longest remaining section of the  berlin wall  for decades the wall divided the city  and germany after it came down artists  from around the world came to paint the  east side of this remaining segment the  gallery became a major tourist  attraction and a symbol of freedom and  human rights  I remember watching when they first  knocked it down  so I mean more than the graffiti the  historical meaning is more important I  think in some ways I think it's  important to remind what happened here  so I think it's very like representative  and all these artists not just art it's  it has a deeper meaning t Renoir was one  of the artists involved he was there  when the project got underway in  February 1990 Susan Matheson is a tempie  between March and September 1990 artists  from around the world came here to  Berlin to paint the wall there was a lot  of euphoria everyone thought freedom is  coming everything will be better  everything will be great a new  documentary film follows some of the  more than 100 artists from 21 countries  who flocked to the stretch of wall  running along the ship lay River they  wanted their artworks to express the joy  they felt after the wall came down  the film also explores the restoration  that took place in 2009 to repair the  damage caused by vandalism and erosion  it also looks at the ongoing conflicts  between artists investors residents and  Berlin's city planners the East Side  Gallery is on prime real estate with a  view of the river that's made it very  attractive to developers  this arenas development plan called for  a part of the wall to be knocked down  even though it was supposed to be a  protected structure I doubt anyone wants  the east side gallery to disappear we  need a suitable urban planning concept  that's Berlin's job and as the federal  government will help finance it a symbol  of freedom or a free-for-all for  developers for now this cultural icon is  here to stay
 

